3. NoSQL Data Model
The application uses two main MongoDB collections in the campus_events database:
campus_events
├── users
└── events

3.1 Users Collection
A user document contains:
_id
firstName
lastName
email
department
role
interests[]
createdAt
The interests field is represented as an array because one user can have multiple interests. A unique index is created on email to prevent duplicate users.

3.2 Events Collection
An event document contains:
_id
title
description
category
tags[]
startDate
endDate
capacity
location
organizerId
registrations[]
createdAt
The location is embedded directly inside the event:
location
├── building
├── room
└── campus
Registrations are also embedded:
registrations[]
├── userId
├── registeredAt
└── status
The event organizer is represented through organizerId, which references a document in the users collection.

3.3 Modeling Decisions
Embedding location: Location is embedded because building, room and campus belong directly to an event and are normally displayed together with the event. Embedding avoids the need for a separate location collection and additional queries.
Embedding registrations: Registrations are embedded within events because registration information is closely associated with a specific event. This also allows MongoDB array operations such as $push and $pull to add and remove registrations directly.
Referencing users: Users are stored separately because users exist independently from events and can participate in multiple events. The registration document therefore stores userId rather than duplicating the complete user information.
Referencing the organizer: organizerId references the corresponding user rather than embedding the entire organizer document.

