5. Application Pages
5.1 Events Page
The Events page displays the complete event list and provides title search, category filtering, tag filtering, upcoming/past filtering, date sorting, capacity indicators, confirmed registration count, create, edit, delete and event details.

5.2 Event Details & Registrations
The Event Details page displays the complete event information, including description, category, tags, dates, location, organizer, capacity and occupancy.
Users can be selected from existing users and registered for an event. The application prevents duplicate registrations and rejects confirmed registrations when the event has reached its capacity.
Registrations are updated directly in the embedded registrations array using MongoDB array operators such as $push and $pull.

5.3 Users Page
The Users page provides a searchable directory of campus users. It supports search by name or email, department filtering, role filtering, registration count, user creation, editing, deletion and user details.
The user details page displays the user's information, interests and registered events. It also separates upcoming and past registrations. Users with zero registrations are supported.

5.5 Analytics Page
The Analytics page contains six MongoDB aggregation analyses required by the project:
Analysis	Purpose
A. Registrations by Category:	Events and confirmed registrations by category
B. Top Five Events:	Ranks events by confirmed registrations and occupancy
C. Users with No Registration:	Identifies users without event registrations
D. Above Average Occupancy:	Finds events above average occupancy
E. Most Used Tags:	Counts tag usage with array aggregation
F. Events by Month:	Groups events and registrations by month

6. MongoDB Aggregation Focus
6.1 Users with No Registration
The users-with-no-registration analysis uses $lookup to connect users with event registrations. The pipeline compares the independent users collection with registration information stored inside event documents.
users
  ↓
$lookup
  ↓
match registration references
  ↓
identify users without matches
  ↓
$project
  ↓
$sort
This analysis is useful for identifying users who have not yet participated in any campus event.

6.2 Events Above Average Occupancy
The occupancy analysis first calculates confirmed registrations for each event and derives an occupancy percentage:
confirmed registrations / capacity × 100
The aggregation then calculates the average occupancy across events and compares each event against that average. The pipeline demonstrates multiple MongoDB aggregation stages, including $filter, $group, $avg, $addFields, $match and $project.

7. Validation and Indexing
MongoDB schema validation was implemented for both collections. Examples of validation rules include required fields, valid email format, unique email addresses, controlled user roles, non-empty event titles, capacity greater than zero, valid event dates, controlled registration statuses and correct embedded document structures.
The following indexes are created:
users.email
events.startDate
events.category
The email index is unique. These indexes support common operations such as finding users by email, sorting events by date and filtering events by category.

8. Difficulties and Limitations
One of the main challenges was designing a MongoDB model that correctly balanced embedded documents and references.
Another challenge was implementing registration rules while keeping registration information embedded within events. The application needed to prevent duplicate registrations and prevent confirmed registrations when an event was full.
The project currently uses a local MongoDB instance and a simple Flask interface. It does not include authentication, cloud deployment or advanced role-based access control.

